are included in VibeCut, and none were translated line by line. VibeCut's
overlay code was written independently.

Ideas taken from its public design and documentation, reimplemented from
scratch for vlogs:
- a fixed, curated library of overlay kinds; the machine only picks which
  one, and when, rather than generating graphics -> web/backend/overlays.py
  (KINDS, suggest);
- the whole set of overlays as one JSON document that can be diffed and
  edited field by field -> overlays live on the storyboard as ops;
- a checker that only reports and never rewrites (too-brief, overlapping,
  cut-off overlays) -> overlays.check;
- a fade that completes before an overlay's end, so it never vanishes on
  a hard cut -> web/frontend/src/overlayDraw.ts (envelope);
- export as frames rendered by the browser and composited by ffmpeg ->
  web/backend/export.py, overlayDraw.renderSchedule (VibeCut renders in the
  creator's own browser with Canvas; no headless browser or puppeteer).

Nunito (web/frontend, via @fontsource/nunito) — SIL Open Font License 1.1.

hypit — https://github.com/hypit-ai/hypit

Idea only: learning an edit's structure from a reference video and
reusing it with other material. No code, templates or assets are included,
and its generative half (swapping in generated footage, faces or voices) is
deliberately not adopted. Here a reference is only *measured*
(cutgrammar/style.py) and applied to the creator's own footage
(web/backend/restyle.py, reference.py, export.py).

